Give the BSDs a default password manager.

password_manager.cc typedefs DefaultPasswordManager in two arms, one for
Linux and WASM and one for Windows and macOS, and nothing else.  On the BSDs
neither arm is taken, so the name is undeclared where the singleton is built
and the compile stops at base/password_manager.cc:285.

The BSDs have no keychain of their own, so they take the plain text manager
Linux takes.  CI only.

--- a/base/password_manager.cc
+++ b/base/password_manager.cc
@@ -269,9 +269,12 @@
 // We use plain text file for password storage on Linux. If you port this module
 // to other Linux distro, you might want to implement a new password manager
 // which adopts some secure mechanism such like gnome-keyring.
-#if defined(__linux__) || defined(__wasm__)
+#if defined(__linux__) || defined(__wasm__) || defined(__FreeBSD__) || \
+    defined(__NetBSD__) || defined(__OpenBSD__) || defined(__DragonFly__)
+// The BSDs have no keychain of their own, so they take the same plain
+// manager Linux takes.
 typedef PlainPasswordManager DefaultPasswordManager;
-#endif  // __linux__ || __wasm__
+#endif  // __linux__ || __wasm__ || the BSDs
 
 // Windows or Mac
 #if (defined(_WIN32) || defined(__APPLE__))
